<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlotte Stanley, Countess of Derby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1599">1599</a>–<a href="/years/1664">1664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Charlotte de La Trémoille</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlotte Stanley, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, born Charlotte de La Trémoille, was a French Huguenot noblewoman who married James Stanley, later 7th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, in 1626. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> she held Lathom House against a parliamentary siege in 1644. She later defended the Isle of Man, which was surrendered to Parliament in 1651, shortly after her husband's execution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlotte-stanley-countess-of-derby.jpg" alt="Charlotte Stanley, Countess of Derby" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte_de_la_Tr%C3%A9mo%C3%AFlle_(1599%E2%80%931664),_Lady_Strange,_Later_Countess_of_Derby.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charlotte de La Trémoille was born at the chateau of Thouars in Poitou, the daughter of Claude de La Trémoille, 2nd Duke of Thouars, and Countess Charlotte Brabantina of Nassau. Her maternal grandparents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William I, Prince of Orange</a>, and Charlotte de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. She married James Stanley on 26 June 1626 and was naturalised as an English subject by Act of Parliament in February 1629. The couple had four daughters and six sons, of whom only five appear to have reached marriageable age, among them Charles Stanley, 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>While her husband, a Royalist commander, was away, she was left in charge of Lathom House, which became the last Royalist stronghold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>. After the fall of Warrington she refused to acknowledge Parliament's authority and surrender the house, saying it would dishonour her husband, but offered to confine herself to defending her home, which delayed an attack. In February 1644 the forces of Sir Thomas Fairfax laid siege. She had fortified the house against bombardment and gathered a militia of seasoned marksmen whose sniping caused heavy losses, and she refused repeated offers of surrender until Prince Rupert relieved the siege on 27 May 1644.</p><p>She and her household were then evacuated to the Isle of Man, of which her husband was Lord. Her attempt to barter the island for his freedom after his capture provoked a revolt led by Illiam Dhone. With the Royalist army destroyed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> and her husband beheaded at Bolton, she held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castle-rushen">Castle Rushen</a> at Castletown until it yielded to Colonel Duckenfield in October 1651.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> wrote that she kept the glory of being the last person in the three kingdoms to submit to the victorious rebels, though the claim is incorrect: the last holdout against Cromwellian forces was the island of Inishbofin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a>, in 1653. Her defence of Lathom House was commemorated by Letitia Elizabeth Landon in a poem of 1833 written for a portrait of Lord and Lady <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> after van Dyck.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charlotte Stanley, Countess of Derby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby, held it for Charles I, and his wife, <b>Charlotte de la Tremoille</b>, defended it until 1651.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Thouars, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Ormskirk, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.97,-0.22&amp;year=1641" aria-label="Château de Thouars on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4545 514 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charlotte Stanley, Countess of Derby"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.97,-0.22&amp;year=1641">What was here in 1641? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
